# The skip allowlist. ONE file, in kit, for the whole fleet.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#
# An entry permits ONE test to be absent from a required tier's expected run.
# Without this file, a test that cannot be proven present is either silently
# tolerated (the failure this repo exists to prevent) or the tier is simply not
# required yet (the same failure, one step earlier).
#
# A JUnit report cannot express "filtered out", so the absence has to be recorded
# somewhere a machine can read. This is that somewhere.
#
# WHY ONE FILE HERE AND NOT ONE PER SERVICE REPO
#
# Thirteen drifting copies of a list of tests is thirteen lists nobody reads in
# aggregate, and the aggregate is the actual problem. Individual entries look
# justified — that is what makes them dangerous. See the total rule below.
#
# A repo that cannot read kit's copy at run time (no submodule, no network in
# the test step) is a real cost of this decision and is called out in
# templates/tier/README.md rather than papered over with a generated second copy.
# A generated copy is a drifting copy with extra steps.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOUR HYGIENE RULES
#
# 1. EVERY ENTRY NAMES A REASON.
#    Without one, "flaky" becomes the reason for everything, and a reason that
#    means nothing cannot be reviewed, expired, or argued with.
#
# 2. EVERY ENTRY NAMES AN OWNER.
#    A skip nobody owns is a skip nobody will ever remove. The owner is a person
#    or a team, not a distribution list.
#
# 3. EVERY ENTRY HAS AN EXPIRY (until) AND A since DATE.
#    The packet requires "an expiry or since date"; this file requires BOTH, and
#    the extra strictness is deliberate. `since` is the ratchet's history — it
#    dates the decision. `until` is the ratchet itself — an entry that cannot
#    expire is an entry that has stopped being a decision and become a fact.
#
#    THE GATE READS THE CLOCK. An entry whose `until` has passed is a FAILURE,
#    not a warning, on the day it passes. That is the design: this file is
#    expected to go red roughly four times a year, and each time the correct
#    response is to delete the entry or to move the date with a new reason. A
#    gate that has never gone red is a report.
#
# 4. AN ENTRY THAT MATCHES NOTHING IS A FAILURE.
#    This is the rule the rest of the file exists to make enforceable, and it is
#    borrowed from ESLint's `reportUnusedDisableDirectives`, which reports a
#    disable comment that no longer suppresses anything. Without it, this file is
#    a ratchet that only turns one way: fixed tests stay listed, listed tests
#    stop being checked, and within two quarters the list contains every test in
#    the repository. That is the failure mode. The unused entry is how you find
#    out first.
#
# THE TOTAL IS PRINTED, PROMINENTLY, ON EVERY RUN.
#
# Every entry individually looks justified. The aggregate is the problem, and an
# aggregate nobody is shown is an aggregate nobody watches. Conftest reports
# exceptions as a separate tally for exactly this reason. The gate prints the
# count on PASS as well as on FAIL, so the number is visible in a green run —
# a green run is where a growing list is least likely to be noticed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT
#
#   skipped <tier> <suite-id> <test-id> reason="…" owner=… since=YYYY-MM-DD until=YYYY-MM-DD
#
# The first four fields are the normalised `skipped` result line from
# templates/tier/README.md, verbatim, so a result and its exemption are the same
# shape. <suite-id> is the language-relative file the declaration lives in; the
# gate resolves <test-id> against the declared inventory and fails when it is
# absent, which is rule 4.
#
# A `#` starts a comment. Blank lines are ignored. One entry per line, and the
# line must be one line: a wrapped reason is two entries, one of which is a
# parse error.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# bun has no declared-reason channel and its filtered-vs-skipped distinction is an
# empty child element rather than a documented field, so nothing machine-readable
# can be required of it yet. Verified on bun 1.3.12, not a documented contract.
skipped db bun/tier.test.ts tier_db_pool_round_trips reason="bun's JUnit reporter carries no declared reason (test.skip takes none) and distinguishes filtered-out from skipped by an undocumented empty element" owner=kit since=2026-09-30 until=2026-12-31

# minitest 6.0.6 ships no machine-readable reporter at all: StatisticsReporter,
# ProgressReporter and SummaryReporter are the complete set, and all three are
# human-readable text. Rails' default runner is minitest, so this is the Rails
# case too. The adapter is a Minitest::Reporter subclass and is unwritten.
skipped db ruby/tier.rb test_tier_db_pool_round_trips reason="minitest has no built-in machine-readable reporter; the tier is declared but nothing can require the test to be present until the reporter adapter is written" owner=kit since=2026-09-30 until=2026-12-31

# ExUnit has no tier-declaration channel and no machine-readable inventory; a
# module attribute is declared in tier.ex and an adapter would read it, but the
# adapter is unwritten.
skipped db elixir/tier.ex tier_db_pool_round_trips reason="ExUnit reports tags to a human, not to a machine; the tier is declared and the collector adapter is unwritten" owner=kit since=2026-09-30 until=2026-12-31
